\label{tab:shape}
\end{table}

Over all $\ShapeRows$ computed rows, $V_f/\Sigma_{\MT}$ lies in $[\ShapeMin,\ShapeMax]$. At $x=2.56\cdot10^8$ it differs from $1$ by at most $\ShapeDevLargest$. So the exact main term of \cref{thm:main}(ii) accounts for the whole ratio dependence seen in \cref{tab:largest}. This agreement is much better than the proved error term $x^{5/3+\eps}+x^{1/2}Y$, which at these sizes is not even smaller than the main term. The pair-count errors $D_x(hq)$ evidently cancel substantially when summed over $h$, which the proof does not exploit. The quantity $N_h$ approaches $C_f$ as $y$ grows, as \cref{prop:poly}(a) predicts. For $k^3+2$ the convergence is slower and less regular, because $y$ is only a few hundred and the error is of order $y^{-1/2}$. The difference $N_{\MT}-N_h$ is the secondary term of the sawtooth sums. It increases with $r$ and vanishes as $r\to0$, consistent with the bound $(Y/x)^{1/6}$ of \cref{prop:MT}, though it appears to behave roughly like a multiple of $\sqrt r$ (\cref{fig:shape}).

