sections/04-identity.tex:55:\Cref{prop:identity} says that the size of $V(x;q)$, about $\sqrt{xq}$, comes from the lattice-point discrepancy $B(X)$ in $\sum_{j\le X}(X-j)=\frac12(X^2-X)+\frac12B(X)$. It does not come from any irregularity in the distribution of squarefree numbers. The terms with $X=x/(qm_t(q))$ of order $1$, that is, $t\approx\sqrt{x/q}$, contribute most, which explains the factor $\sqrt{x/q}$; see \cref{sec:sawtooth}. In the computations of \cref{sec:numerics} the identity was also checked numerically: with every other term computed by brute force, it holds at $x=3000$ for all $q\le40$ to a relative accuracy of $\SelfTest$ (\texttt{reproduce.py}, function \texttt{identity\_selftest}).
