sections/10-numerics.tex:134:which follow from~\eqref{eq:euler} as in \cref{lem:eta}. The first $64$ terms of the subtracted partial sums are evaluated in $80$-digit arithmetic; $A_0$ and $\prod_pa_p$ are evaluated to high precision through the prime zeta function. The infinite series~\eqref{eq:MT} is thus evaluated without truncation, up to floating-point rounding. As an independent check, \cref{prop:identity} was verified with this implementation of $\MT$ against brute-force values of every other term, for $x=3000$ and all $q\le40$ (largest relative discrepancy $\SelfTest$). For $2k+1$ the number of moduli is about $x/2$, so only $x\le10^6$ was treated.
